// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Precalculus is about understanding functions deeply: their shapes, behaviors and connections. Let's start with the essentials.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A function assigns exactly one output to each input. Key features to analyze:"},{"t":"list","items":["Domain and range, written in interval notation like [−2, 5) or (−∞, 3].","Intercepts, increasing and decreasing intervals, and relative maximums and minimums.","Symmetry: even functions satisfy f(−x) = f(x) (symmetric about the y-axis); odd functions satisfy f(−x) = −f(x) (symmetric about the origin)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Average rate of change on [a, b] = (f(b) − f(a)) ÷ (b − a), the slope of the secant line."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Is f(x) = x³ − 4x even, odd or neither?","steps":["f(−x) = (−x)³ − 4(−x) = −x³ + 4x","That equals −(x³ − 4x) = −f(x)"],"answer":"Odd"}]},{"say":"Transformations, compositions and inverses let you build complicated functions from simple ones and run them backward.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"y = a·f(b(x − h)) + k: h shifts horizontally, k vertically, |a| stretches vertically, |b| compresses horizontally by a factor of 1/|b|, and negative a or b reflect."},{"t":"p","text":"Composition (f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x)). The domain of f ∘ g includes only x-values in g's domain whose outputs are in f's domain."},{"t":"p","text":"To find an inverse: swap x and y and solve for y. A function has an inverse function only if it's one-to-one (passes the horizontal line test). The graphs of f and f⁻¹ reflect across y = x."},{"t":"ex","problem":"f(x) = √x and g(x) = x − 4. Find (f ∘ g)(x) and its domain.","steps":["f(g(x)) = √(x − 4)","Need x − 4 ≥ 0"],"answer":"√(x − 4), domain [4, ∞)"}]}],[{"say":"Polynomials are smooth, continuous curves. Their degree, leading coefficient and zeros tell you their whole shape.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"End behavior depends on the degree and leading coefficient. A degree-n polynomial has at most n real zeros and at most n − 1 turning points."},{"t":"key","text":"Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: a degree-n polynomial has exactly n complex zeros (counting multiplicity). Complex zeros of real polynomials come in conjugate pairs."},{"t":"p","text":"Tools for finding zeros: the Rational Root Theorem, synthetic division, the Factor Theorem, and factoring."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A polynomial with real coefficients has zeros 2 and 3 + i. Name another zero, and the lowest possible degree.","steps":["Complex zeros come in conjugate pairs: 3 − i"],"answer":"3 − i is also a zero; the lowest degree is 3"},{"t":"p","text":"Multiplicity: at a zero with odd multiplicity the graph crosses the x-axis; with even multiplicity it touches and turns."}]},{"say":"Rational functions have asymptotes and holes that create dramatic graphs. Let's learn to sketch them by analysis.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"For f(x) = p(x)/q(x) (in lowest terms):"},{"t":"list","items":["Vertical asymptotes at zeros of q.","Holes where a factor cancels.","Horizontal asymptote by comparing degrees; if the top's degree is exactly one more, there's a slant (oblique) asymptote found by long division."]},{"t":"key","text":"To solve polynomial or rational inequalities, find the critical values (zeros and undefined points), make a sign chart, and test each interval."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Solve (x − 2)/(x + 1) ≥ 0.","steps":["Critical values: x = 2 (zero) and x = −1 (undefined)","Test intervals: x < −1 positive; −1 < x < 2 negative; x > 2 positive"],"answer":"(−∞, −1) ∪ [2, ∞)"}]}],[{"say":"Exponentials and logs are inverses. Together they model growth, decay, sound, earthquakes and more.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"y = bˣ and y = log_b x are inverse functions. log_b x = y means bʸ = x."},{"t":"p","text":"Properties: log(MN) = log M + log N; log(M/N) = log M − log N; log(Mᵏ) = k log M; change of base log_b x = ln x / ln b."},{"t":"p","text":"Graphs: y = bˣ has a horizontal asymptote y = 0 and passes through (0, 1). y = log_b x has a vertical asymptote x = 0 and passes through (1, 0)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Condense 2 ln x + ln 5 − ln y into one logarithm.","steps":["2 ln x = ln x²","ln x² + ln 5 = ln 5x²"],"answer":"ln(5x²/y)"}]},{"say":"Exponential models describe real change. Solving them means using logs to bring exponents down.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"To solve exponential equations: isolate the exponential, take a log of both sides, and use the power rule. To solve log equations: condense, rewrite in exponential form, and check for extraneous solutions."},{"t":"key","text":"Continuous growth: A = Pe^(rt). Compound interest: A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt). Half-life and doubling time come from setting A = P/2 or A = 2P."},{"t":"ex","problem":"How long does it take $1,000 to double at 6% compounded continuously?","steps":["2000 = 1000e^(0.06t), so 2 = e^(0.06t)","ln 2 = 0.06t"],"answer":"t = ln 2 / 0.06 ≈ 11.6 years"},{"t":"p","text":"Logistic growth models populations that level off at a carrying capacity."}]}],[{"say":"The unit circle is the key to all of trigonometry. Master it and everything else follows.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"On the unit circle, a point at angle θ has coordinates (cos θ, sin θ), and tan θ = sin θ / cos θ."},{"t":"p","text":"Radians: 2π = 360°. Key angles: π/6 (30°), π/4 (45°), π/3 (60°), π/2 (90°)."},{"t":"p","text":"Exact values in Quadrant I: sin π/6 = 1/2, sin π/4 = √2/2, sin π/3 = √3/2 (and cosine reverses this order)."},{"t":"p","text":"Use reference angles and quadrant signs (All Students Take Calculus) for other angles. Reciprocal functions: csc = 1/sin, sec = 1/cos, cot = 1/tan."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the exact value of tan(4π/3).","steps":["4π/3 is in Quadrant III with reference angle π/3","tan π/3 = √3, and tangent is positive in Quadrant III"],"answer":"√3"}]},{"say":"Trig functions repeat, so they're perfect for modeling cycles. Inverse trig functions find angles from ratios.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"y = A sin(B(x − C)) + D: amplitude |A|, period 2π/|B|, phase shift C, midline y = D."},{"t":"p","text":"Tangent has period π and vertical asymptotes where cos x = 0. Secant and cosecant have asymptotes where cos and sin are zero."},{"t":"p","text":"Inverse trig functions undo trig functions on restricted domains:"},{"t":"list","items":["arcsin x: outputs in [−π/2, π/2].","arccos x: outputs in [0, π].","arctan x: outputs in (−π/2, π/2)."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Evaluate arccos(−1/2).","steps":["Which angle in [0, π] has cosine −1/2?"],"answer":"2π/3"},{"t":"p","text":"Modeling: a Ferris wheel, tides or daylight hours can be modeled by a sinusoid by finding the amplitude, midline and period from the data."}]}],[{"say":"Trig identities let you rewrite expressions in simpler or more useful forms. They're essential for calculus.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Pythagorean identities: sin²θ + cos²θ = 1, 1 + tan²θ = sec²θ, 1 + cot²θ = csc²θ."},{"t":"p","text":"Other key identities:"},{"t":"list","items":["Sum and difference: sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B; cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B.","Double angle: sin 2θ = 2 sin θ cos θ; cos 2θ = cos²θ − sin²θ = 1 − 2sin²θ = 2cos²θ − 1."]},{"t":"p","text":"To verify an identity, work on one side only: rewrite in terms of sine and cosine, factor, or use Pythagorean identities."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the exact value of sin 75°.","steps":["sin(45° + 30°) = sin 45° cos 30° + cos 45° sin 30°","(√2/2)(√3/2) + (√2/2)(1/2)"],"answer":"(√6 + √2)/4"}]},{"say":"Trig equations have infinitely many solutions because trig functions repeat. And the laws of sines and cosines solve any triangle.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"To solve a trig equation: isolate the trig function, find solutions in one period, then add multiples of the period for all solutions."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Solve 2cos x + 1 = 0 on [0, 2π).","steps":["cos x = −1/2"],"answer":"x = 2π/3 or 4π/3"},{"t":"key","text":"Law of Sines: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C. Law of Cosines: c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C."},{"t":"p","text":"Use the Law of Sines for AAS, ASA and SSA (watch for the ambiguous case with two possible triangles). Use the Law of Cosines for SAS and SSS."},{"t":"p","text":"Area of a triangle with two sides and the included angle: Area = ½ab sin C."}]}],[{"say":"Vectors have both size and direction, making them perfect for forces, velocities and navigation.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A vector can be written as ⟨a, b⟩ or ai + bj. Its magnitude is |v| = √(a² + b²), and its direction angle θ satisfies tan θ = b/a."},{"t":"list","items":["Add and subtract component by component.","Scalar multiplication stretches a vector: 3⟨1, 2⟩ = ⟨3, 6⟩.","A unit vector has magnitude 1: v/|v|."]},{"t":"key","text":"Dot product: u · v = u₁v₁ + u₂v₂ = |u||v|cos θ. If u · v = 0, the vectors are perpendicular (orthogonal)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the angle between u = ⟨1, 0⟩ and v = ⟨1, 1⟩.","steps":["u · v = 1; |u| = 1; |v| = √2","cos θ = 1/√2"],"answer":"θ = 45°"}]},{"say":"Parametric and polar equations describe curves that ordinary y = f(x) functions can't, like circles traced over time and flower shapes.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Parametric equations give x and y in terms of a parameter t: x = f(t), y = g(t). They describe motion, showing position and direction over time. To eliminate the parameter, solve one equation for t and substitute."},{"t":"p","text":"Polar coordinates (r, θ) locate a point by distance from the origin and angle."},{"t":"key","text":"Converting: x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ, r² = x² + y², tan θ = y/x."},{"t":"p","text":"Polar graphs include circles (r = a), roses (r = a cos nθ), cardioids (r = a + a cos θ) and limaçons."},{"t":"p","text":"Complex numbers in polar form: a + bi = r(cos θ + i sin θ). De Moivre's theorem: [r(cos θ + i sin θ)]ⁿ = rⁿ(cos nθ + i sin nθ)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Convert the polar point (4, π/3) to rectangular coordinates.","steps":["x = 4 cos(π/3) = 2","y = 4 sin(π/3) = 2√3"],"answer":"(2, 2√3)"}]}],[{"say":"Sequences and series show how patterns grow, and the binomial theorem expands powers without endless multiplication.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Arithmetic sequences: aₙ = a₁ + (n − 1)d, Sₙ = n(a₁ + aₙ)/2. Geometric: aₙ = a₁rⁿ⁻¹, Sₙ = a₁(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r). An infinite geometric series converges to a₁/(1 − r) when |r| < 1."},{"t":"p","text":"Mathematical induction proves a statement for all natural numbers: prove a base case, then prove that if it's true for k, it's true for k + 1."},{"t":"key","text":"Binomial theorem: (a + b)ⁿ = Σ C(n, k) aⁿ⁻ᵏ bᵏ. The coefficients come from Pascal's triangle."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the coefficient of x² in (x + 2)⁵.","steps":["The term with x² has k = 3: C(5, 3) x² 2³","10 × 8 = 80"],"answer":"80"}]},{"say":"Limits are the doorway to calculus. They describe what a function approaches, even if it never gets there.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"lim (x→c) f(x) = L means f(x) gets arbitrarily close to L as x approaches c. The limit exists only if the left- and right-hand limits are equal."},{"t":"key","text":"Evaluate limits by direct substitution first. If you get 0/0, factor and cancel, rationalize, or simplify."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find lim (x→4) (x² − 16)/(x − 4).","steps":["Substitution gives 0/0","Factor: (x + 4)(x − 4)/(x − 4) = x + 4"],"answer":"8"},{"t":"p","text":"Limits at infinity describe end behavior: for rational functions, compare degrees. A limit that grows without bound is written as ∞ (the limit does not exist as a real number)."}]}]];
export default text;
